186124353 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124353 #edb/ref 1071 "There are two groups of people in a particular city: those who use their own vehicles and those who use public transport. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport, and\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles.\n\nGive the stochastic matrix that describes how people change their means of transportation each year." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124353 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e8f30d47-bfd2-50ea-b2ad-d474e2a58d29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124353 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the following situation for each year: \n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport. This implies that $65\\%$ of them remain using their own vehicles.\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles. This implies that $75\\%$ of them remain using public transport.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Vehicles** | **Public Transport ** |\n| To: | **Vehicles** | $0.65$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Public Transport** | $0.35$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n![](images/b2/b21fee1abe56dcfdc687be14278c62a20d201ff00e96f1e5918ce22d9dc8b03c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124354 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5599" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124354 #edb/ref 1071 "A certain industry produces $2$ types of sugar, namely sugar $A$ and sugar $B$. The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of sugar in a month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Sugar $A$** | **Sugar $B$** |\n| To: | **Sugar $A$** | $0.3$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sugar $B$** | $0.7$ | $0.55$ |  |\n\nThat is, $70\\%$ of sugar $A$ customers switch to sugar $B$ each month, and $45\\%$ of sugar $B$ customers switch to sugar $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600$ customers use sugar $A,$ while $400$ customers use sugar $B.$ How many customers will use sugar $A$ in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124354 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "95a45716-8c29-5955-af3c-43b6344912da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124354 #edb/ref 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n![](images/45/451b82e8cb00ede7b0ddf73f35c0555d0eeeeefc5beb5ce4fddd54f0df5f2577.png)\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of sugar is\n![](images/56/56952aeed1d718033af8a3e6d09e8e269f1d70aa8dc31609b8289f025f2e6bd6.png)\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n![](images/ca/cae1bed6b8b01b3ff66c2e8bacf7a10f6c0ece53f3aeac0fb1a51e8fc3c07e8e.png)\n\nTo find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_1$ after one month, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1=P\\mathbf{x}_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.55\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.36 \\\\ 0.64\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $\\color{blue}36\\%$ of customers will use sugar $A.$ Computing $36\\%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000 \\cdot 0.36 = 360.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $360$ customers will use sugar $A$ in one month." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124363 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5971" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124363 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/16/165917feacd28344502759fc6e03f96c5b54d9530701916b746fc489374a6d0b.png) \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector $\\mathbf{x}_0$ given above. Calculate the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124363 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09540ff7-0444-5169-b582-471be2f1972d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124363 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2,$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x_1}=P\\mathbf{x}_0, \\qquad \\mathbf{x}_2=P\\mathbf{x}_1\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.172 \\\\ 0.828\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124425 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9162" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124425 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n![](images/70/70ee929c71d89a443d3ea0efc39ed225f344685059b86a04285a118a476da0f8.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124425 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25622a0e-3e5b-5f09-bef6-afaa30d153d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124425 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{U}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries): \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0 \\\\ 1.2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{V}$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}0.35 & 0.95 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.05\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\begin{aligned}0.35+0.65=1 \\\\ 0.95+0.05=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{W}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its third column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\bf{V}$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124544 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124544 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124544 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124544 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124544 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124544 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124544 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124544 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124544 #edb/ref 1039 "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix Given Some Data" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124544 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124544 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "abbdd778-7ace-5539-9d6f-cdf01804b237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124545 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124545 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124545 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124545 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124545 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124545 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124545 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124545 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124545 #edb/ref 1039 "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix and Making a Prediction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124545 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124545 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3e93dde4-a8e8-548a-801e-3d9a320a4513" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124554 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124554 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124554 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124554 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124554 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124554 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124554 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124554 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124554 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124554 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a State Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124554 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124554 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4821de45-1b6b-5b4f-be0e-96cf5395bc8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124614 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124614 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124614 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124614 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124614 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124614 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124614 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124614 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124614 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Stochastic Vectors and Stochastic Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124614 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124614 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "66b24a98-c641-5e08-8bec-30f221d1bb22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124711 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124711 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124711 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124711 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124711 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124711 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124711 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124711 #edb/ref 1045 "Markov Chains" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124711 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124711 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124711 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e3965249-6bb4-5bb3-b83d-3689a8e9f9e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124768 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124768 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109881" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124768 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124768 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0.5\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.45 \\\\ 1.55\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124768 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d6371d4-1a60-5526-b7b1-69a38ead3549" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124768 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our vectors in turn.\n\n- Vector $u$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.5+0+0.5=1\n$$\n\n- Vector $v$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $w$ is *not* a stochastic vector since the entries do not add up to one:\n\n$$\n0+0=0\\ne 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$u$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124769 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124769 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124769 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$U = \\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, V = \\begin{bmatrix}0.45 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.55 & 0.75\\end{bmatrix}, W = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124769 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d2f0627-b325-5745-b223-6d5747b6cfb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124913 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124913 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35791" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124913 #edb/ref 1071 "There are three groups of fans in a certain town: those who prefer basketball, those who prefer baseball, and those who prefer football. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- from the people that prefer basketball, $32%$ change to baseball and $28%$ change to football,\n- from the people that prefer baseball, $45%$ change to football and $15%$ change to basketball,\n- from the people that prefer football, $25%$ change to basketball and $65%$ change to baseball.\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a + b + c$?\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.15 & a \\\\ 0.32 & b & 0.65 \\\\ c & 0.45 & 0.1\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124913 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f54c1f29-b43d-5f0e-a558-1fd0e7509f92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124922 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124922 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35842" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124922 #edb/ref 1071 "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}, x_{0} = \\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.6 \\\\ 0.2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector $x_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector $x_{1}$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124922 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27eeb3bb-6a91-5ba8-9145-47e0dfe56bb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124922 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124924 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124924 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124924 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124924 #edb/ref 1071 "A certain company produces two types of smartphones: Phone $A$ and Phone $B.$ The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of smartphones each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Phone $A$** | **Phone $B$** |\n| To: | **Phone $A$** | $0.6$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Phone $B$** | $0.4$ | $0.7$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40%$ of Phone $A$ customers switch to Phone $B$ each year, and $30%$ of Phone $B$ customers switch to Phone $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600\\,000$ customers use Phone $A,$ while $400\\,000$ customers use Phone $B.$ How many customers will use Phone $A$ in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124924 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62389cec-4c75-591c-8976-ec4ed362e628" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124924 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124924 #edb/ref 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each smartphone is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}600\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}\\begin{bmatrix}600\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.48 \\\\ 0.52\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $48%$ of customers will use Phone $A.$ Computing $48%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.48=480\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $480\\,000$ customers will use Phone $A$ in one year." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124925 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124925 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124925 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35846" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124925 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124925 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124925 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3da8136-058f-5b0b-abbd-f2c6d4a902e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124925 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124925 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the state vector ${x}_{2},$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0},\\,{x}_{2}=P{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.29 \\\\ 0.71\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.29 \\\\ 0.71\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.413 \\\\ 0.587\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125443 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125443 #edb/ref 1061 20288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125443 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f00b242-2400-5948-9531-618af1db558d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125443 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125444 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125444 #edb/ref 1061 20289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125444 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ccc7d00-22a8-5d1a-a0d0-a3e6b10db6f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125444 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125445 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125445 #edb/ref 1061 20290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125445 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "730b29bf-923b-5d88-9f79-57bed7ca1ca8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125445 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125446 #edb/ref 1061 20291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125446 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bf64f3e5-2318-517c-9967-386760b0f779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125446 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125457 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125457 #edb/ref 1061 22210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125457 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c314ef44-f421-5bc5-b410-ab965ccc6457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125457 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125564 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125564 #edb/ref 1061 34164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125564 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d7e8a13c-5f02-5712-9c41-daa1892a5092" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125564 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125800 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use repeated matrix multiplication to determine the state of a system given its initial state and some information about how one state transitions to the next state.\n\nTo explain this concretely, suppose we have a population where people live either in the city or the countryside. A study carried out by the local authority shows that, each year,\n\n- $6\\%$ of the city population relocates to the countryside,\n\n- $3\\%$ of the countryside population relocates to the city, and\n\n- everyone else remains where they are.\n\nThis information can be represented using a diagram, like the one below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab6b3cecac2af22b8665078db56c7daf492c0381434c9c88fe21f2630f743466.png)\n\nWe can also present this information as a table:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **City** | **Countryside** |\n| To: | **City** | $0.94$ | $0.03$ |\n| **Countryside** | $0.06$ | $0.97$ |  |\n\nWe want to use this information to determine how the population will be distributed $n$ years after the study was carried out. \n\nFirst, let's turn this table into a matrix $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this matrix has the following properties:\n\n- it is a square matrix\n\n- the sum of the entries in each column equals $1$\n\n- all entries in the matrix are non-negative\n\nA square matrix with these properties is called a **stochastic matrix**.\n\nNow suppose that, at the time of the study, $70\\%$ of the population lived in the city and $30\\%$ in the countryside. We can represent this **initial state** using a so-called **stochastic vector** $\\mathbf x_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.70 \\\\ 0.30\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStochastic vectors contain non-negative entries only, and the sum of the entries equals $1.$ Note that each column of $P$ is a stochastic vector.\n\nNow that we have our stochastic matrix and initial state vector, we can use matrix multiplication to calculate the population distribution sometime after the initial study took place:\n\n- To calculate the population distribution after one year, we calculate the product $P\\mathbf x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe resulting vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ntells us that one year after the study, $66.7$% of the population will live in the city, and $33.3$% will live in the countryside.\n\n- To model the distribution after $2$ years, we compute $P\\mathbf x_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe resulting vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.637 \\\\ 0.363\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ntells us that two years after the study, $63.7$% of the population will live in the city, and $36.3$% will live in the countryside." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125800 #edb/ref 1065 3645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125800 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0d87aed7-76da-5126-8eee-f38ced9a46f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125800 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125801 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's reconsider the population we looked at earlier, where the stochastic vector that represents the initial distribution of people between the city and the countryside is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.70 \\\\ 0.30\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand the yearly change in this distribution is represented by the stochastic matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEarlier, we found that the population distribution after $1$ and $2$ years is given by the vectors $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2,$ \n respectively, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{x}_0, \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots$ are called **state vectors**, and $\\mathbf x_0$ is the **initial state**. \n\nTo find the state vector corresponding to the $(k+1)$th year, we left-multiply the state vector corresponding to the $k$th year by $P.$ This gives rise to the following sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sequence of stochastic vectors $\\mathbf{x}_0, \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots$ related to a stochastic matrix $P$ in this way is called a **Markov chain**." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125801 #edb/ref 1065 3646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125801 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f6720cd1-1b8b-5b03-9092-c29474fc606d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125801 #edb/ref 1067 "Markov Chains" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244331 #edb/ref 1080 "$u$, $v$, and $w$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244331 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244331 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "029787b4-76ef-5324-83eb-649b506a66cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244559 #edb/ref 1080 "$u$ and $v$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244559 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244559 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1bc43066-87ae-5d14-b7df-35e4b5a96b4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244592 #edb/ref 1080 "$v$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244592 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244592 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1f880ee7-2dae-5e94-a00e-cf7ea06127d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244953 #edb/ref 1080 "$u$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244953 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244953 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "47d8a976-4e1b-5d5c-9400-17de622336ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245215 #edb/ref 1080 "$u$ and $w$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245215 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245215 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "66317c76-eb73-56c5-a0e6-948b81d5904d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246777 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246777